<!-- Pulled into directional-derivatives.ptx by XInclude.
     Requires in assets/ :  isotherm-runner.html, isotherm-runner-preview.png -->

<section xml:id="subsec-scalar-fields-potentials">
  <title>Scalar Fields, Potentials, and the Direction Nature Chooses</title>

  <p>
    A <term>scalar field</term> is a rule that assigns a single number to every point of
    a region: the temperature <m>T(x,y)</m> in a city, the pressure <m>P(x,y)</m> over an
    ocean, the elevation <m>h(x,y)</m> on a hillside, the concentration <m>c(x,y)</m> of a
    pollutant in a corridor. A scalar field carries no direction of its own. It is only a
    number at each location.
  </p>
  
  <p>
  Physics, by contrast, is full of quantities that point somewhere: heat flows,
   forces push, wind blows. These are <term>vector fields</term>. The gradient is
   what ties them back to scalar fields <mdash/> at least for the
   <term>conservative</term> fields taken up in MATH 14 at SCU, where the whole
   vector field is recovered from a single scalar potential by differentiation.
   Because such fields are so common in nature, the pattern in the table below
   appears again and again across physics.
  </p>

  <p>
    When a scalar field governs a physical force or flux, we call the field a
    <term>potential</term>, and the associated vector quantity is, up to a constant, the
    <em>negative</em> gradient of that potential.
  </p>

  <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor">
    <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
      <cell>Setting</cell>
      <cell>Scalar field</cell>
      <cell>Vector quantity</cell>
      <cell>Level curves are</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>Heat conduction</cell>
      <cell>temperature <m>T</m></cell>
      <cell>heat flux <m>\mathbf q = -k\nabla T</m> (Fourier's law)</cell>
      <cell>isotherms</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>Electrostatics</cell>
      <cell>potential <m>V</m></cell>
      <cell>electric field <m>\mathbf E = -\nabla V</m></cell>
      <cell>equipotentials</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>Mechanics</cell>
      <cell>potential energy <m>U</m></cell>
      <cell>conservative force <m>\mathbf F = -\nabla U</m></cell>
      <cell>contours of constant energy</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>Meteorology</cell>
      <cell>pressure <m>P</m></cell>
      <cell>pressure-gradient force <m>-\nabla P</m> per unit volume</cell>
      <cell>isobars</cell>
    </row>
  </tabular>

  <p>
    The minus sign in each of these laws encodes one physical fact:
    <em>nature moves downhill</em>. Heat drains from hot to cold, charges move toward low
    electric potential, a ball rolls toward low gravitational potential energy, air accelerates 
    toward low pressure. So the direction the physics actually selects is the direction of most rapid
    <em>decrease</em>, which is exactly the case <m>\theta = \pi</m> of
    <m>D_{\mathbf u}f = |\nabla f|\cos\theta</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Three consequences carry over unchanged from
    <xref ref="sec-dirderiv-properties"/>, and they are worth restating in physical
    language.
  </p>

  <p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>\theta = 0</m>: fastest increase, at the rate <m>|\nabla f|</m>. This is the
        direction heat comes <em>from</em>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>\theta = \pi</m>: fastest decrease, at the rate <m>-|\nabla f|</m>. This is the
        direction the flux, the force, or the wind actually points.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>\theta = \pi/2</m>: no change at all. These directions trace the level curves of
        the field <mdash/> isotherms, equipotentials, isobars, the contour lines on a
        topographic map. Since <m>\nabla f</m> is orthogonal to the level curve through a
        point, <em>the flux, the force, or the wind always crosses the
        contours at right angles</em>.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>
  </p>

<section xml:id="sec-Examples-from-Mechanics">
  <title>Examples from Mechanics</title>
  
<p>
  You may recall in your introductory physics course that the gravitational potential energy <m>U</m> of a mass <m>m</m> at height <m>y</m> is
  <m>U = mgy</m>.  When the height remains constant, the potential energy is constant.  The surface of constant potential energy is
  level surface - or an equipotential surface.

  Using the potential, the gravitational force is
  <md>
    <mrow> {\mathbf F} \amp = -\nabla U </mrow>
    <mrow> \amp = -\frac{d}{dy}(mgy) \hat{\mathbf j}</mrow>
    <mrow> \amp = -mg{\hat{\mathbf j}} </mrow>
  </md>
  which points straight down.  Note that the force is perpendicular to the level surface of constant potential energy.
</p>

<p>
  <xref ref="fig-gravity-potential-gradient"/> shows the two halves of this statement side by
  side. On the left is the physical picture: the mass, its height above the ground, and the
  horizontal surfaces on which <m>U = mgy</m> does not change. On the right the same surfaces
  are drawn again, now carrying the gradient and the force.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-gravity-potential-gradient">
  <caption>
    Gravitational potential energy near the earth's surface.
    <em>Left:</em> a mass <m>m</m> at heights <m>y_1 \lt y_2 \lt y_3</m>. Since <m>U = mgy</m>
    depends on the height alone, each dashed horizontal line is an equipotential surface: every
    point on it carries the same potential energy.
    <em>Right:</em> at every point <m>\nabla U = mg\,\hat{\mathbf j}</m> points straight up,
    the direction in which <m>U</m> increases fastest, and the force
    <m>\mathbf F = -\nabla U = -mg\,\hat{\mathbf j}</m> points straight down. Both are
    perpendicular to the equipotential surfaces, as the right angles record.
  </caption>
  <sidebyside widths="48% 48%" margins="2%" valign="middle">
    <figure xml:id="fig-gravity-height">
      <caption>The mass and its height above the surface of the earth.</caption>
      <image xml:id="image-gravity-height" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>
          A mass at height y above the ground, with three dashed horizontal lines of constant
          gravitational potential energy.
        </shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>
            The surface of the earth is drawn as a hatched horizontal line at the bottom, and a
            vertical axis labeled <m>y</m> measures height above it. Three dashed blue horizontal
            lines are drawn at heights <m>y_1</m>, <m>y_2</m>, and <m>y_3</m>, labeled
            <m>U = mgy_1</m>, <m>U = mgy_2</m>, and <m>U = mgy_3</m>; these are the equipotential
            surfaces, and higher lines carry larger potential energy. A small vermillion ball of
            mass <m>m</m> rests on the middle line, and a double-headed arrow from the ground up
            to the ball marks its height <m>y_2</m>. A box at the top records
            <m>U = mgy</m>.
          </p>
        </description>
        <latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[>={Latex}, line join=round, line cap=round]
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oivermillion}{RGB}{213,94,0}
  % ---- surface of the earth ----
  \fill[black!8] (-0.4,-0.45) rectangle (6.3,0);
  \draw[black!70, line width=1pt] (-0.4,0) -- (6.3,0);
  \foreach \i in {-0.1,0.3,...,6.3} {
    \draw[black!40, line width=0.5pt] (\i,0) -- (\i-0.3,-0.32);
  }
  \node[black!70, font=\small, anchor=north] at (2.9,-0.5) {surface of the earth};
  % ---- height axis ----
  \draw[->, black!75, line width=0.7pt] (0.5,-0.05) -- (0.5,4.55)
    node[anchor=south, font=\small] {$y$};
  % ---- equipotential surfaces: U = mgy is constant on each ----
  \draw[oiblue, dashed, line width=0.9pt] (0.15,1.2) -- (4.6,1.2)
    node[anchor=west, oiblue, font=\small] {$U = mgy_1$};
  \draw[oiblue, dashed, line width=0.9pt] (0.15,2.4) -- (4.6,2.4)
    node[anchor=west, oiblue, font=\small] {$U = mgy_2$};
  \draw[oiblue, dashed, line width=0.9pt] (0.15,3.6) -- (4.6,3.6)
    node[anchor=west, oiblue, font=\small] {$U = mgy_3$};
  % ---- ticks on the height axis ----
  \draw[black!75, line width=0.7pt] (0.38,1.2) -- (0.62,1.2);
  \node[black!75, font=\small, anchor=east] at (0.34,1.2) {$y_1$};
  \draw[black!75, line width=0.7pt] (0.38,2.4) -- (0.62,2.4);
  \node[black!75, font=\small, anchor=east] at (0.34,2.4) {$y_2$};
  \draw[black!75, line width=0.7pt] (0.38,3.6) -- (0.62,3.6);
  \node[black!75, font=\small, anchor=east] at (0.34,3.6) {$y_3$};
  % ---- the object and its height ----
  \draw[<->, black!75, line width=0.7pt] (2.9,0.03) -- (2.9,2.22)
    node[pos=0.72, fill=white, inner sep=1.5pt, font=\small] {$y_2$};
  \fill[oivermillion] (2.9,2.4) circle (0.17);
  \draw[black!60, line width=0.5pt] (2.9,2.4) circle (0.17);
  \node[anchor=south west, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] at (3.02,2.46) {$m$};
  % ---- annotation ----
  \node[anchor=north east, draw=black!30, rounded corners=2pt, fill=white,
        inner sep=4pt, font=\small] at (6.3,5.4) {$U = mgy$};
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <figure xml:id="fig-gravity-force-gradient">
      <caption>
        The gradient, the force, and the equipotential surfaces they cross at right angles.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-gravity-force-gradient" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>
          Upward gradient arrows and downward force arrows meeting the horizontal equipotential
          lines at right angles.
        </shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>
            The same hatched ground and the same three dashed blue horizontal equipotential lines
            appear again. At three points, one on each line, a green arrow points straight up,
            labeled <m>\nabla U</m>, and a vermillion arrow of the same length points straight
            down, labeled <m>\mathbf F = -\nabla U</m>. The arrows are identical at every point,
            because the field is uniform. A small square at each point marks the right angle
            between the arrows and the equipotential line, so the force and the gradient cross
            the level surfaces perpendicularly. A box at the top records
            <m>\nabla U = mg\,\hat{\mathbf j}</m> and
            <m>\mathbf F = -\nabla U = -mg\,\hat{\mathbf j}</m>.
          </p>
        </description>
        <latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[>={Latex}, line join=round, line cap=round]
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oivermillion}{RGB}{213,94,0}
  \definecolor{oigreen}{RGB}{0,158,115}
  % ---- surface of the earth ----
  \fill[black!8] (-0.4,-0.45) rectangle (6.3,0);
  \draw[black!70, line width=1pt] (-0.4,0) -- (6.3,0);
  \foreach \i in {-0.1,0.3,...,6.3} {
    \draw[black!40, line width=0.5pt] (\i,0) -- (\i-0.3,-0.32);
  }
  \node[black!70, font=\small, anchor=north] at (2.9,-0.5) {surface of the earth};
  % ---- height axis ----
  \draw[->, black!75, line width=0.7pt] (0.5,-0.05) -- (0.5,4.55)
    node[anchor=south, font=\small] {$y$};
  % ---- the same equipotential surfaces ----
  \draw[oiblue, dashed, line width=0.9pt] (0.15,1.2) -- (4.6,1.2)
    node[anchor=west, oiblue, font=\small] {$U = mgy_1$};
  \draw[oiblue, dashed, line width=0.9pt] (0.15,2.4) -- (4.6,2.4)
    node[anchor=west, oiblue, font=\small] {$U = mgy_2$};
  \draw[oiblue, dashed, line width=0.9pt] (0.15,3.6) -- (4.6,3.6)
    node[anchor=west, oiblue, font=\small] {$U = mgy_3$};
  % ---- gradient (up) and force (down) at three sample points ----
  % lowest level curve
  \draw[->, oigreen, line width=1.3pt] (1.4,1.2) -- (1.4,1.85);
  \draw[->, oivermillion, line width=1.3pt] (1.4,1.2) -- (1.4,0.55);
  \draw[black!55, line width=0.6pt] (1.4,0.98) -- (1.62,0.98) -- (1.62,1.2);
  % middle level curve, where the mass sits
  \fill[black!25] (2.9,2.4) circle (0.15);
  \draw[black!45, line width=0.5pt] (2.9,2.4) circle (0.15);
  \draw[->, oigreen, line width=1.3pt] (2.9,2.4) -- (2.9,3.05)
    node[anchor=west, oigreen, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] {$\nabla U$};
  \draw[->, oivermillion, line width=1.3pt] (2.9,2.4) -- (2.9,1.75)
    node[anchor=west, oivermillion, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] {$\mathbf F = -\nabla U$};
  \draw[black!55, line width=0.6pt] (2.9,2.18) -- (3.12,2.18) -- (3.12,2.4);
  % highest level curve
  \draw[->, oigreen, line width=1.3pt] (4.1,3.6) -- (4.1,4.25);
  \draw[->, oivermillion, line width=1.3pt] (4.1,3.6) -- (4.1,2.95);
  \draw[black!55, line width=0.6pt] (4.1,3.38) -- (4.32,3.38) -- (4.32,3.6);
  % ---- annotation ----
  \node[anchor=north east, draw=black!30, rounded corners=2pt, fill=white,
        inner sep=4pt, font=\small, align=left] at (6.3,5.4)
    {$\nabla U = mg\,\hat{\mathbf j}$\\[2pt]
     $\mathbf F = -\nabla U = -mg\,\hat{\mathbf j}$};
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </sidebyside>
</figure>

<p>
Using this same idea we can also look at the potential energy of a spring.  
The potential energy of a spring is given by <m>U = \frac{1}{2}kx^2</m> where <m>k</m> is the spring constant and <m>x</m> is 
the displacement from equilibrium.  The force exerted by the spring is given by

<md>
    <mrow> {\mathbf F} \amp = -\nabla U </mrow>
    <mrow> \amp = -\frac{d}{dx}\left(\frac{1}{2}kx^2\right) \hat{\mathbf i}</mrow>
    <mrow> \amp = -kx{\hat{\mathbf i}} </mrow>
  </md>
more commonly known as Hooke's Law.  Again, the force is perpendicular to the level surface of constant potential energy.
</p>

<p>
  <xref ref="fig-spring-potential-gradient"/> tells the same story for the spring. Here the
  potential energy depends only on <m>x</m>, so its level curves in the plane are the vertical
  lines <m>x = \text{constant}</m>, and the force, which is horizontal, meets every one of them
  at a right angle.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-spring-potential-gradient">
  <caption>
    A block on a spring.
    <em>Left:</em> the block displaced a distance <m>x</m> from equilibrium, drawn above the
    graph of its potential energy <m>U = \frac{1}{2}kx^2</m>; the two pictures share the same
    horizontal scale, so the block sits directly above its own point on the parabola.
    <em>Right:</em> because <m>U</m> depends on <m>x</m> alone, the equipotential curves are the
    vertical lines <m>x = \text{constant}</m>. The gradient
    <m>\nabla U = kx\,\hat{\mathbf i}</m> points away from equilibrium, the force
    <m>\mathbf F = -kx\,\hat{\mathbf i}</m> points back toward it, and both cross the
    equipotential lines at right angles. The arrows are twice as long on the outer lines
    because <m>|\nabla U| = k|x|</m> grows with the displacement.
  </caption>
  <sidebyside widths="48% 48%" margins="2%" valign="middle">
    <figure xml:id="fig-spring-displacement">
      <caption>
        The block, its displacement from equilibrium, and the potential energy
        <m>U = \frac{1}{2}kx^2</m>.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-spring-displacement" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>
          A block on a horizontal spring displaced from equilibrium, drawn above the parabola
          giving its potential energy.
        </shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>
            In the upper half of the picture a spring runs from a hatched wall on the left to a
            block of mass <m>m</m> resting on a hatched floor. A dashed gray vertical line marks
            the equilibrium position, labeled <m>x = 0</m>, and a second dashed vertical line
            passes through the block, which has been pulled to the right; a double-headed arrow
            between the two lines is labeled <m>x</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            In the lower half, on the same horizontal scale, the parabola
            <m>U = \frac{1}{2}kx^2</m> is graphed in blue, with its vertex at the equilibrium
            position. The vertical axis is labeled <m>U</m> and the horizontal axis is labeled
            <m>x</m>. The dashed line through the block continues downward and meets the
            parabola at a marked point, the potential energy stored in the spring at that
            displacement. The parabola is symmetric, so stretching and compressing by the same
            amount store the same energy.
          </p>
        </description>
        <latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[>={Latex}, line join=round, line cap=round]
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oivermillion}{RGB}{213,94,0}
  % ================= the physical spring =================
  % ---- wall ----
  \fill[black!8] (-0.45,0) rectangle (0,1.7);
  \draw[black!70, line width=1pt] (0,0) -- (0,1.7);
  \foreach \i in {0.35,0.65,...,1.7} {
    \draw[black!40, line width=0.5pt] (0,\i) -- (-0.35,\i-0.28);
  }
  % ---- floor ----
  \draw[black!70, line width=0.9pt] (-0.45,0) -- (5.3,0);
  \foreach \i in {-0.15,0.25,...,5.25} {
    \draw[black!40, line width=0.5pt] (\i,0) -- (\i-0.3,-0.3);
  }
  % ---- spring ----
  \draw[oiblue, line width=1pt, decorate,
        decoration={coil, aspect=0.55, segment length=3.2mm, amplitude=2.2mm}]
    (0,0.5) -- (3.65,0.5);
  % ---- block, displaced to the right of equilibrium ----
  \fill[oivermillion!85] (3.65,0) rectangle (4.35,0.95);
  \draw[black!65, line width=0.6pt] (3.65,0) rectangle (4.35,0.95);
  \node[white, font=\small] at (4.0,0.48) {$m$};
  % ---- equilibrium line and displaced line, shared by both pictures ----
  \draw[black!55, dashed, line width=0.7pt] (3.0,1.55) -- (3.0,-2.35);
  \node[black!65, font=\small, anchor=south] at (3.0,1.58) {$x = 0$};
  \draw[black!55, dashed, line width=0.7pt] (4.0,1.25) -- (4.0,-1.6);
  % ---- the displacement ----
  \draw[<->, black!75, line width=0.7pt] (3.0,1.25) -- (4.0,1.25)
    node[midway, fill=white, inner sep=1.5pt, font=\small] {$x$};
  % ================= the potential energy graph =================
  \draw[->, black!75, line width=0.7pt] (1.2,-2.2) -- (5.0,-2.2)
    node[anchor=north east, font=\small] {$x$};
  \draw[->, black!75, line width=0.7pt] (3.0,-2.35) -- (3.0,-0.75)
    node[anchor=east, font=\small, inner sep=3pt] {$U$};
  \draw[oiblue, line width=1.2pt]
    plot[domain=1.4:4.6, samples=80] ({\x},{-2.2+0.6*(\x-3)*(\x-3)});
  \node[oiblue, font=\small, anchor=west] at (4.68,-1.0) {$U = \frac{1}{2}kx^2$};
  % ---- the block's own point on the parabola ----
  \fill[oivermillion] (4.0,-1.6) circle (0.075);
  \draw[black!55, dashed, line width=0.6pt] (3.0,-1.6) -- (4.0,-1.6);
  \node[black!75, font=\small, anchor=east] at (2.92,-1.6) {$\frac{1}{2}kx^2$};
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <figure xml:id="fig-spring-force-gradient">
      <caption>
        The gradient and the force, orthogonal to the equipotential lines
        <m>x = \text{constant}</m>.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-spring-force-gradient" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>
          Vertical equipotential lines of the spring potential, with horizontal gradient arrows
          pointing outward and force arrows pointing back toward equilibrium.
        </shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>
            A pair of coordinate axes is drawn, with the equilibrium position of the spring at
            the origin. Four dashed blue vertical lines at <m>x = \pm a</m> and
            <m>x = \pm 2a</m> are the equipotential curves of <m>U = \frac{1}{2}kx^2</m>, the
            outer pair carrying four times the energy of the inner pair.
          </p>
          <p>
            At a point on each line a horizontal green arrow labeled <m>\nabla U</m> points away
            from the origin and a horizontal vermillion arrow labeled
            <m>\mathbf F = -\nabla U</m> points back toward it. The arrows on the outer lines
            are twice as long as those on the inner lines, since <m>|\nabla U| = k|x|</m>. A
            small square at each point marks the right angle between the horizontal arrows and
            the vertical equipotential line.
          </p>
        </description>
        <latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[>={Latex}, line join=round, line cap=round, scale=1.15]
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oivermillion}{RGB}{213,94,0}
  \definecolor{oigreen}{RGB}{0,158,115}
  % ---- axes, with the equilibrium position at the origin ----
  \draw[->, black!75, line width=0.7pt] (-3.4,0) -- (3.5,0)
    node[anchor=north east, font=\small] {$x$};
  \draw[->, black!75, line width=0.7pt] (0,-1.9) -- (0,2.45)
    node[anchor=south east, font=\small] {$y$};
  % ---- equipotential curves: U = (1/2)k x^2 is constant on each vertical line ----
  \draw[oiblue, dashed, line width=0.9pt] (1.1,-1.75) -- (1.1,1.85);
  \draw[oiblue, dashed, line width=0.9pt] (-1.1,-1.75) -- (-1.1,1.85)
    node[anchor=south, oiblue, font=\small] {$U = \frac{1}{2}ka^2$};
  \draw[oiblue, dashed, line width=0.9pt] (-2.2,-1.75) -- (-2.2,1.85);
  \draw[oiblue, dashed, line width=0.9pt] (2.2,-1.75) -- (2.2,1.85)
    node[anchor=south, oiblue, font=\small] {$U = 2ka^2$};
  \node[black!70, font=\small, anchor=north] at (1.1,-1.8) {$a$};
  \node[black!70, font=\small, anchor=north] at (2.2,-1.8) {$2a$};
  \node[black!70, font=\small, anchor=north] at (-1.1,-1.8) {$-a$};
  \node[black!70, font=\small, anchor=north] at (-2.2,-1.8) {$-2a$};
  % ---- x = a : gradient outward, force inward ----
  \draw[->, oigreen, line width=1.3pt] (1.1,0.8) -- (1.6,0.8);
  \draw[->, oivermillion, line width=1.3pt] (1.1,0.8) -- (0.6,0.8);
  \draw[black!55, line width=0.6pt] (1.28,0.8) -- (1.28,0.62) -- (1.1,0.62);
  % ---- x = 2a : twice as long, since |grad U| = k|x| ----
  \draw[->, oigreen, line width=1.3pt] (2.2,-0.8) -- (3.2,-0.8)
    node[anchor=west, oigreen, font=\small, inner sep=3pt] {$\nabla U$};
  \draw[->, oivermillion, line width=1.3pt] (2.2,-0.8) -- (1.2,-0.8);
  \node[oivermillion, font=\small, anchor=north, fill=white, inner sep=2pt] at (1.7,-1.02)
    {$\mathbf F = -\nabla U$};
  \draw[black!55, line width=0.6pt] (2.38,-0.8) -- (2.38,-0.98) -- (2.2,-0.98);
  % ---- x = -a ----
  \draw[->, oigreen, line width=1.3pt] (-1.1,-0.8) -- (-1.6,-0.8);
  \draw[->, oivermillion, line width=1.3pt] (-1.1,-0.8) -- (-0.6,-0.8);
  \draw[black!55, line width=0.6pt] (-1.28,-0.8) -- (-1.28,-0.98) -- (-1.1,-0.98);
  % ---- x = -2a ----
  \draw[->, oigreen, line width=1.3pt] (-2.2,0.8) -- (-3.2,0.8);
  \draw[->, oivermillion, line width=1.3pt] (-2.2,0.8) -- (-1.2,0.8);
  \draw[black!55, line width=0.6pt] (-2.38,0.8) -- (-2.38,0.62) -- (-2.2,0.62);
  % ---- annotation ----
  \node[anchor=north, draw=black!30, rounded corners=2pt, fill=white,
        inner sep=4pt, font=\small, align=left] at (0,-2.35)
    {$\nabla U = kx\,\hat{\mathbf i}$\\[2pt]
     $\mathbf F = -\nabla U = -kx\,\hat{\mathbf i}$ \quad (Hooke's Law)};
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </sidebyside>
</figure>


</section>


  <example xml:id="ex-two-runners">
    <title>Two Runners in a City Heat Island</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        On a still summer afternoon the pavement downtown is far hotter than the outlying
        neighborhoods. Model the surface air temperature, in degrees Fahrenheit, by
        <md>T(x,y) = 74 + 14\,e^{-(x^2 + 3y^2)/40},</md>
        where <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are measured in miles east and north of downtown. The
        core reaches <m>88^\circ</m>F, and the outskirts settle near <m>74^\circ</m>F. Two
        runners meet at the point <m>P_0(1,1)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              The first runner is overheating and wants cooler air as quickly as possible.
              Which direction should she take, and how fast does the temperature fall along
              it?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The second runner has settled into the temperature at <m>P_0</m> and wants a
              route on which it never changes at all. What path should she run?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Differentiating the exponential with the chain rule,
        <md>
          <mrow>\nabla T \amp = -\frac{14}{40}\,e^{-(x^2+3y^2)/40}\left(2x\,\mathbf i + 6y\,\mathbf j\right),</mrow>
          <mrow>(\nabla T)_{(1,1)} \amp = -\frac{14\,e^{-1/10}}{40}\left(2\,\mathbf i + 6\,\mathbf j\right)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp \approx -0.633\,\mathbf i - 1.900\,\mathbf j .</mrow>
        </md>
        At <m>P_0</m> the temperature itself is
        <m>T(1,1) = 74 + 14e^{-1/10} \approx 86.7^\circ</m>F.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part 1.</term> Temperature falls fastest in the direction of
        <m>-\nabla T</m>, which is a positive multiple of <m>\mathbf i + 3\mathbf j</m>.
        Normalizing, the heading is north-northeast:
        <md>\mathbf u = \frac{1}{\sqrt{10}}\,\mathbf i + \frac{3}{\sqrt{10}}\,\mathbf j,</md>
        and the rate of change along it is
        <md>
          <mrow>D_{\mathbf u} T \amp = -|\nabla T|
                = -\frac{14\,e^{-1/10}}{40}\sqrt{4 + 36}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp \approx -2.0 \ \frac{{}^\circ\text{F}}{\text{mi}} .</mrow>
        </md>
        Notice how modest this is. The gradient hands her the <em>best available</em>
        direction, not a dramatic one: even running the optimal heading, she must cover a
        full mile to shed about two degrees. Any other heading does worse.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part 2.</term> The second runner needs <m>D_{\mathbf u}T = 0</m>, that is,
        <m>\mathbf u</m> orthogonal to <m>\nabla T</m>:
        <md>\mathbf u = \pm\frac{1}{\sqrt{10}}\left(3\,\mathbf i - \mathbf j\right).</md>
        Following that condition continuously, rather than for a single step, means never
        leaving the level curve of <m>T</m> through <m>P_0</m>. Since <m>T</m> is constant
        exactly when <m>x^2 + 3y^2</m> is constant, her route is
        <md>x^2 + 3y^2 = 4,</md>
        an ellipse with semi-axes <m>2</m> miles east-west and
        <m>2/\sqrt{3} \approx 1.15</m> miles north-south <mdash/> a closed loop of roughly
        ten miles, every step of it at <m>86.7^\circ</m>F.
      </p>

    <p>
      In the animation below, switch between the two runners. On the loop the thermometer
      holds at <m>86.7^\circ</m>F while the blue gradient arrow stays stubbornly
      perpendicular to her heading; on the escape route the same reading falls steadily, and
      the path cuts across every isotherm at a right angle.
    </p>
      <p>
        Use the interactive figure to explore the problem further geometrically,
        switching between the two routes and watching how the readings respond as the
        runner moves.
      </p>
    <figure xml:id="figure-isotherm-runner">
      <caption>
        Two routes from <m>P_0(1,1)</m> in the field
        <m>T(x,y) = 74 + 14e^{-(x^2+3y^2)/40}</m>. The highlighted ellipse is the
        level curve <m>x^2+3y^2=4</m>, on which <m>D_{\mathbf u}T = 0</m>.
      </caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-isotherm-runner"
                   iframe="isotherm-runner.html"
                   width="100%" aspect="6:5">
        <instructions>
          <p>
            Press <q>Run the isotherm</q> to send the runner around the level
            curve <m>x^2+3y^2=4</m>, or <q>Dash for cool air</q> to send her
            along the path of steepest cooling. The readout shows her
            temperature, position, directional derivative
            <m>D_{\mathbf u}T</m>, and distance travelled; the blue arrow is
            <m>\nabla T</m> at her position. Use <q>Pace</q> to change her
            speed, and <q>Pause</q> or <q>Reset</q> to control the animation.
          </p>
        </instructions>
      </interactive>
    </figure>
    </solution>
  </example>


</section>
